  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A slow moving cold front approaches tonight and \ncrosses the area Friday. \n\nAn upper-level trough pivoting over the Great Lakes region will\ncontinue to slowly track eastward through tomorrow, before the \nupper low moves overhead on Saturday. At the surface, this \ntranslates to a slow moving cold front approaching the region \nthis evening, before slowly moving overhead tonight and \nstalling across parts of our area tomorrow.\n\nIsolated to widely scattered showers this evening will become\nmore widespread overnight. Since there has been a lack of rain \nfor our area the past 2 to 3 weeks, the showers are rather \nbeneficial. Rainfall amounts will vary across the area with \nlower amounts N/W (a tenth to three- quarters of an inch) while\nsouthern NJ and Delmarva will have higher totals of 1 to 2 \ninches. The rain is expected to occur over a long enough period \nto preclude any flooding concerns. A few thunderstorms may \ndevelop this evening or overnight, but the chances of this are \nlow. The front stalls for a period tomorrow morning before \nresuming a SE push during the afternoon. This will promote the \nhigher QPF totals for those areas. Conditions will gradually \nimprove throughout the day tomorrow with mostly fair weather for\nareas N/W. There will be slower improvement for S/E areas. For the\nI-95 corridor, conditions are forecast to improve during the\nafternoon. The SE areas may not see any substantial improvement\nuntil the evening hours.\n\nThe cold front departs offshore Friday night, with dry conditions \nreturning for much of the area. However, guidance indicates an \nupper- low will track eastward across northern portions of the \narea on Saturday. This will lead to a lingering chance for some \nlight rain north of I-78, with the highest chances (40-50%) \nacross the Poconos and far northern NJ. By Sunday morning, the \nupper-low will be east of the area bringing a return of dry \nconditions to the region along with cooler temperatures.",
